//! Go `openrtb_ext`: Prebid extension types, bidder names and `RequestWrapper`.
//!
//! Every exported type of the package's request, imp, bid, response, user, device, site, app,
//! regs, source, publisher, supply chain, deal tier, multibid, floors, alternate bidder code,
//! video and bidder params files is generated by `pbs-devtools typegen` (`typegen.json`) into
//! `generated.rs`; the types with Go JSON methods, the named string and map types, and the
//! package's functions are written by hand, one module per Go file. Everything is exported at
//! the crate root, as Go exports it from the one package (the `openrtb_ext` module names the same
//! items, for paths spelled after the Go package).
//!
//! # JSON
//!
//! Every type has two JSON codes. The `GoJson` code (`pbs_gojson`) reproduces jsoniter and
//! `encoding/json` for the callers that still use it, until it is removed. The serde code
//! (`Serialize`, `Deserialize` and `pbs_jsonutil::MergeClone`) is what the crate itself uses: the
//! request wrapper, [`convert_up_to26`], [`convert_down_to25`], [`read_deal_tiers_from_imp`] and
//! [`parse_device_ext_atts`] read and write JSON with `serde_json`, through pbs-jsonutil where Go
//! calls `util/jsonutil`. typegen generates it for the generated types; the types with Go JSON
//! methods (`ExtSite`, `ExtDeviceInt`, `ExtImpAppnexusKeywords`, `PriceGranularity`,
//! `StringOrStringArray`) implement it by hand after their Go methods, reading the members Go's
//! jsonparser finds through small borrowed structs; the named string and map types delegate to what
//! they hold, and `ImpExtEpom`, a struct without fields that typegen does not write, reads any
//! object. Where the serde code differs from Go, `differences.toml` and the ledgers of pbs-jsonutil
//! and pbs-openrtb name it; `tests/parity/shapes.rs` and `tests/parity/serde_goldens.rs` hold it to
//! Go's goldens.
//!
//! # Differences from Go
//!
//! * Go's named string types `BidderName`, `BidType`, `TargetingKey`, `CookieStatus` and
//!   `UserSyncType` hold a `Cow<'static, str>`, so that Go's constants are Rust constants.
//! * Where Go walks a map in its random order and the result depends on it
//!   (`ExtImpAppnexusKeywords` from an object, colliding names in [`read_deal_tiers_from_imp`],
//!   several case-insensitive matches in
//!   [`ExtAlternateBidderCodes::is_bidder_in_alternate_bidder_codes`]), Rust walks the keys in byte
//!   order: always the same result, and one Go can give, except that the keywords of an object with
//!   three keys or more can come in an order Go's walk never gives (`json.keywords-object-order`).
//! * Go's nil receivers and nil elements that would panic are handled without panicking (each
//!   function documents what it does instead).
//! * Copies own their data: where a Go `Clone` or `DeepCopy` shares a map, slice or pointer with
//!   the original, the Rust copy has its own, which differs only when one side is changed later.
//! * Errors that Go returns as `errortypes` values are [`pbs_errortypes::GoError`]s; errors of a Go
//!   `UnmarshalJSON` are [`pbs_gojson::JsonError`]s, which keep only their text (as
//!   `jsonutil.Unmarshal` does).
//! * Go strings are Rust `String`s: invalid UTF-8 that jsoniter keeps in a Go string is U+FFFD.
//! * Bidder names are lower-cased and case-folded by std's tables, a later Unicode version than
//!   go1.26.2's 15.0.0 (`config.bidder-name-unicode`), and the errors of
//!   [`ExtAlternateBidderCodes::is_valid_bidder_code`] quote names as Rust quotes strings, where Go
//!   uses `strconv.Quote` (`config.alternate-code-quoting`).
//! * `encoding/json` (not jsoniter, which the server uses for these types) decoding a literal other
//!   than `null` into a nil `*[]T` field (`ImpExtConcert.Sizes`) leaves an empty slice where Go
//!   leaves nil: `pbs_gojson`'s slices do not keep nil there.

